{"id":845,"date":"2012-07-14T18:15:13","date_gmt":"2012-07-14T18:15:13","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/?page_id=845"},"modified":"2023-10-19T18:02:56","modified_gmt":"2023-10-19T22:02:56","slug":"politics-and-civility","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/applying-moral-psych\/politics-and-civility\/","title":{"rendered":"To Politics and Civility"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"attachment_123\" style=\"width: 310px\" class=\"wp-caption alignright\"><a href=\"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0993.jpg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-123\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-123\" title=\"IMG_0993\" src=\"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0993-300x204.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"204\" srcset=\"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0993-300x204.jpg 300w, https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0993.jpg 697w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px\" \/><\/a><p id=\"caption-attachment-123\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Tea partiers use the Liberty\/oppression foundation (photo by Emily Ekins)<\/p><\/div>\n<p>I&#8217;ll have a lot more here eventually, as various people and groups find ways to apply moral psychology to improving cross-partisan dialogue and relationships.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>1) I co-run a project to apply moral psych to political civility at <a title=\"CivilPolitics.org\" href=\"http:\/\/www.civilpolitics.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">www.CivilPolitics.org<\/a>. See especially these <a href=\"http:\/\/www.civilpolitics.org\/content\/understanding-other-side\">links to videos, books, and online essays<\/a> that can help liberals and conservatives understand each other.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>2) There are many groups working to foster greater understanding across the political divide, often by building personal relationships, which I believe is the best way to proceed (because intuitions come first, strategic reasoning second, so change the intuitions before you ask people to reason with each other). Those groups include:<\/p>\n<p>&#8212;The<a href=\"http:\/\/www.livingroomconversations.org\/\">\u00a0Livingroom Conversations Project<\/a><\/p>\n<p>&#8212;<a href=\"http:\/\/tothevillagesquare.org\/\">The Village Square<\/a>, a bipartisan group of Floridians who foster dialogue and civility in state politics, in part by hosting events involving shared meals. (Dining together activates tribal psychology that reduces divisions)<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>3) There are many groups working to overcome the gridlock, mistrust, corruption and inefficiency \u00a0that makes American government so ineffective. A few that I really like include:<\/p>\n<p>&#8212;<a href=\"http:\/\/www.nolabels.org\/\">NoLabels.org<\/a><\/p>\n<p>&#8212;<a href=\"http:\/\/www.bipartisanpolicy.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Bipartisan Policy Center<\/a><\/p>\n<p>&#8212;<a href=\"http:\/\/www.commongood.org\/pages\/philip-k.-howard\">CommonGood.org<\/a><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>4) I co-run a project to apply moral psych to business ethics at <a title=\"EthicalSystems.org\" href=\"http:\/\/www.ethicalsystems.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">www.EthicalSystems.org<\/a>. [on hold while I&#8217;m on book tour, but will get going after the election]<\/p>\n<p>5) Here is a page of <a href=\"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/about-the-book\/discussions-of-the-book\/\">links to essays <\/a>that have applied the ideas in the book to addressing social problems.<\/p>\n<p>6) I&#8217;m hopeful that churches, synagogues, Rotary clubs, and other organizations will arrange cross-partisan dinner discussions, asking participants to read <em>The Righteous Mind<\/em> first, and then acting on the last line of the book: &#8220;We\u2019re all stuck here for a while, so let\u2019s try to work it out.&#8221; I&#8217;ll post links to groups that have succeeded in fostering such conversations here, including <a href=\"http:\/\/www.publicconversations.org\/resources\/guides\">The Public Conversations Project<\/a>, <a href=\"http:\/\/tothevillagesquare.org\/\">The Village Square<\/a>, and <a href=\"http:\/\/www.livingroomconversations.org\/\">The Livingroom Conversations Project<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>7) I&#8217;ll note blogs and websites that are applying moral psych in interesting ways:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http:\/\/theindependentwhig.com\/\">The Independent Whig <\/a>(writing about morality and politics from a conservative perspective)<\/li>\n<li><a href=\"http:\/\/econlog.econlib.org\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Econlog<\/a> (Bryan Caplan and Arnold Kling address social and policy issues as libertarian economists)<\/li>\n<li><a href=\"http:\/\/isabel.penraeth.com\/tagged\/moral_foundation_theory\/chrono\">Isabel Penreath<\/a> has been applying and critiquing moral foundations theory from a liberal Christian perspective<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<div id=\"attachment_152\" style=\"width: 305px\" class=\"wp-caption alignnone\"><a href=\"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0766.jpg\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-152\" class=\"size-medium wp-image-152\" title=\"IMG_0766\" src=\"http:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0766-295x300.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"295\" height=\"300\" srcset=\"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0766-295x300.jpg 295w, https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2012\/01\/IMG_0766.jpg 1007w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 295px) 100vw, 295px\" \/><\/a><p id=\"caption-attachment-152\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Occupiers use the Liberty\/oppression foundation<\/p><\/div>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>I&#8217;ll have a lot more here eventually, as various people and groups find ways to apply moral psychology to improving cross-partisan dialogue and relationships. &nbsp; 1) I co-run a project to apply moral psych to political civility at www.CivilPolitics.org. See especially these links to videos, books, and online essays that can help liberals and conservatives [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":8,"featured_media":0,"parent":10,"menu_order":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","template":"page-full.php","meta":{"footnotes":""},"class_list":["post-845","page","type-page","status-publish","hentry"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/845","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/page"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/8"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=845"}],"version-history":[{"count":10,"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/845\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2534,"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/845\/revisions\/2534"}],"up":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/10"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/righteousmind.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=845"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}